            <ArticleTitle>The diagnostic problem of cephalalgia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>382-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cephalalgia, a common complaint, often presents both a diagnostic and a therapeutic problem. In a significant number of cases the cause may be traced to allergic sensitivity. &quot;Allergic headache&quot; is no different from other allergic diseases either as to the factors that produce the head pain or as to the specific treatment required. Taking a careful history and carrying out definitive skin testing may lead to diagnosis in cases that otherwise might be considered functional.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
